199
00:08:24,120 --> 00:08:27,573
I work with AI Education Project or aiEDU for short.

200
00:08:28,470 --> 00:08:31,680
We are a nonprofit organization that works nationally,

201
00:08:31,680 --> 00:08:33,630
primarily with K-12 school districts

202
00:08:33,630 --> 00:08:38,630
to pursue work in light of AI readiness.

203
00:08:39,480 --> 00:08:41,520
Essentially, we partner with school districts,

204
00:08:41,520 --> 00:08:44,130
in some cases county offices of education,

205
00:08:44,130 --> 00:08:45,900
in some cases state departments of education,

206
00:08:45,900 --> 00:08:48,780
in some cases higher education organizations.

207
00:08:48,780 --> 00:08:52,350
Our goal is really to equip teachers,

208
00:08:52,350 --> 00:08:55,200
K-12 systems, and students ultimately

209
00:08:55,200 --> 00:08:56,790
for the skills, capacities,

210
00:08:56,790 --> 00:08:58,740
mindsets that they need to thrive in the world

211
00:08:58,740 --> 00:09:00,240
where AI is quite ubiquitous.

212
00:09:00,240 --> 00:09:02,730
And I know it's like a probably a point

213
00:09:02,730 --> 00:09:07,730
that you've all heard several times in the past day,

214
00:09:07,800 --> 00:09:10,410
but the work that we do is really

215
00:09:10,410 --> 00:09:12,603
to go hand-in-hand with school systems.

216
00:09:13,560 --> 00:09:15,030
I try and talk about our work

217
00:09:15,030 --> 00:09:18,150
in like three primary pillars of work.

218
00:09:18,150 --> 00:09:19,380
The first is defining like,

219
00:09:19,380 --> 00:09:21,480
what the heck does AI readiness even mean?

220
00:09:21,480 --> 00:09:23,340
I know there's a ton of conversations around

221
00:09:23,340 --> 00:09:25,290
like what skills and capacities students need

222
00:09:25,290 --> 00:09:27,480
in order to thrive in this AI world.

223
00:09:27,480 --> 00:09:29,730
We do, yes, define those skills and capacities,

224
00:09:29,730 --> 00:09:33,063
but we try and actually like tease that a little bit more.

225
00:09:34,740 --> 00:09:36,930
We deliver a lot of this transformative work.

226
00:09:36,930 --> 00:09:39,180
So that can range from content development,

227
00:09:39,180 --> 00:09:42,420
that ranges from policy development,

228
00:09:42,420 --> 00:09:45,750
teacher training, guidance, research reports.

229
00:09:45,750 --> 00:09:48,300
We do a lot of that work itself.

230
00:09:48,300 --> 00:09:50,880
And then we also try and catalyze this movement.

231
00:09:50,880 --> 00:09:53,400
We issue grants out to other nonprofit organizations.

232
00:09:53,400 --> 00:09:55,320
We partner with other national movements.

233
00:09:55,320 --> 00:09:58,020
We work with state agencies and governmental bodies

234
00:09:58,020 --> 00:10:00,933
in order to make sure the work is scaled and equitable.

235
00:10:02,730 --> 00:10:05,340
Generally, we try and work deeply within a few states.

236
00:10:05,340 --> 00:10:08,880
We know that as one small national nonprofit organization,

237
00:10:08,880 --> 00:10:10,530
we're about 20 people right now,

238
00:10:10,530 --> 00:10:13,710
we can't work everywhere in as deep as we'd want to,

239
00:10:13,710 --> 00:10:17,610
so we'd rather scale depth rather than scale breadth.

240
00:10:17,610 --> 00:10:19,230
And so we've chosen a few specific states

241
00:10:19,230 --> 00:10:21,840
to do some work in, California, being one of them.

242
00:10:21,840 --> 00:10:24,090
I'm based in California. I was a California teacher.

243
00:10:24,090 --> 00:10:25,680
I love the state so much.

244
00:10:25,680 --> 00:10:27,930
And so like doing a lot of work here is something

245
00:10:27,930 --> 00:10:29,880
that we're really, really dedicated to.

246
00:10:31,260 --> 00:10:33,810
So as we define what AI readiness means, this is what I'm,

247
00:10:33,810 --> 00:10:37,590
like the core of what we think of AI readiness is

248
00:10:37,590 --> 00:10:40,380
and how it's differentiated from AI literacy is

249
00:10:40,380 --> 00:10:42,300
we think of AI literacy as like understanding the basics

250
00:10:42,300 --> 00:10:45,570
of how AI works using AI tools, yada yada.

251
00:10:45,570 --> 00:10:46,770
But we do think that it needs

252
00:10:46,770 --> 00:10:48,660
to be paired with other basic things

253
00:10:48,660 --> 00:10:50,730
that aren't usually defined under AI literacy,

254
00:10:50,730 --> 00:10:53,400
like being a critical thinker, being self-motivated,

255
00:10:53,400 --> 00:10:55,230
being creative, being collaborative.

256
00:10:55,230 --> 00:10:57,780
Those aren't necessary AI literacy skills,

257
00:10:57,780 --> 00:10:59,520
but we do think those are necessary skills

258
00:10:59,520 --> 00:11:01,680
in order for students to be able to thrive in a world

259
00:11:01,680 --> 00:11:03,630
where AI is quite ubiquitous.

260
00:11:03,630 --> 00:11:06,030
And so we also wanna pair that with something that we refer

261
00:11:06,030 --> 00:11:08,490
to as a human advantage and students understanding

262
00:11:08,490 --> 00:11:11,130
and implicitly knowing how to leverage

263
00:11:11,130 --> 00:11:14,010
their human advantage skills in this economy

264
00:11:14,010 --> 00:11:15,270
that's gonna be very disrupted

265
00:11:15,270 --> 00:11:16,740
by technology in the next couple of years

266
00:11:16,740 --> 00:11:19,090
and has already seen that disruption happening.

267
00:11:20,220 --> 00:11:22,560
And we lay out this framework not just for students,

268
00:11:22,560 --> 00:11:26,250
but we also do all of this depth of work for educators.

269
00:11:26,250 --> 00:11:28,590
What do educators and what skills do educators need

270
00:11:28,590 --> 00:11:30,420
to be equipped with?

271
00:11:30,420 --> 00:11:31,920
And then what do systems,

272
00:11:31,920 --> 00:11:34,620
like what are actual the systemic mechanisms

273
00:11:34,620 --> 00:11:36,147
to make this available to all teachers

274
00:11:36,147 --> 00:11:38,880
and all educators and students within that system?

275
00:11:38,880 --> 00:11:40,650
And so we try and lay out this framework

276
00:11:40,650 --> 00:11:42,570
from a individual perspective

277
00:11:42,570 --> 00:11:44,490
scaled up to what the system needs to do

278
00:11:44,490 --> 00:11:46,383
in order to support that individual.

279
00:11:47,640 --> 00:11:49,920
And like I said, we develop a lot of classroom curricula,

280
00:11:49,920 --> 00:11:51,960
we do a lot of teacher professional development.

281
00:11:51,960 --> 00:11:54,210
I know this is probably the change mechanism

282
00:11:54,210 --> 00:11:56,790
that's very difficult to scale within school systems

283
00:11:56,790 --> 00:11:59,040
is how do I upskill my current educators?

284
00:11:59,040 --> 00:12:02,010
How do we make sure that the people working within my system

285
00:12:02,010 --> 00:12:05,400
are able to actually work with this disruption

286
00:12:05,400 --> 00:12:07,470
and are not getting overwhelmed by it?

287
00:12:07,470 --> 00:12:08,910
Investing in the human capital

288
00:12:08,910 --> 00:12:11,250
that you have in your system is probably the first step

289
00:12:11,250 --> 00:12:12,870
that you need to make before you start investing

290
00:12:12,870 --> 00:12:16,230
in the tech capacities within these new systems.

291
00:12:16,230 --> 00:12:17,430
If you do one without the other,

292
00:12:17,430 --> 00:12:18,630
then it's just not gonna work,

293
00:12:18,630 --> 00:12:20,230
making sure that both are paired

294
00:12:21,240 --> 00:12:24,720
with an actual like order of operations

295
00:12:24,720 --> 00:12:27,570
that makes sense and it includes teacher buy-in

296
00:12:27,570 --> 00:12:28,683
is so critical.

297
00:12:30,150 --> 00:12:30,983
And like I said,

298
00:12:30,983 --> 00:12:32,580
we do a lot of work directly with school systems.

299
00:12:32,580 --> 00:12:35,490
It ranges from small K-12 school districts

300
00:12:35,490 --> 00:12:37,860
to the entire state Departments of Education.

301
00:12:37,860 --> 00:12:40,830
Understanding how this works from a big school system

302
00:12:40,830 --> 00:12:42,510
like New York City public schools

303
00:12:42,510 --> 00:12:46,110
down to a small rural district in Northern California,

304
00:12:46,110 --> 00:12:48,660
like understanding what change management looks like

305
00:12:48,660 --> 00:12:51,210
in these different systems is really important to us

306
00:12:51,210 --> 00:12:53,790
to understand that like there is no-one-size-fits-all model

307
00:12:53,790 --> 00:12:55,833
for AI readiness in all these systems.

308
00:12:57,180 --> 00:12:58,680
And then, like I said, we try and catalyze our work,

309
00:12:58,680 --> 00:13:01,293
meaning we develop partnerships, we do research,

310
00:13:02,520 --> 00:13:03,480
we issue a lot of grants.

311
00:13:03,480 --> 00:13:04,500
I think so far we've issued

312
00:13:04,500 --> 00:13:07,290
about $2.5 million in grants to small,

313
00:13:07,290 --> 00:13:09,660
specifically rural and Indigenous nonprofits

314
00:13:09,660 --> 00:13:11,040
across the country

315
00:13:11,040 --> 00:13:13,290
to do this work themselves within their own communities

316
00:13:13,290 --> 00:13:14,970
to make sure that this is something that is built

317
00:13:14,970 --> 00:13:17,433
out of communities and not put into communities.

318
00:13:19,350 --> 00:13:20,403
And a lot of the work that we're doing

319
00:13:20,403 --> 00:13:22,603
with the California Community College system

320
00:13:23,763 --> 00:13:25,350
is in service of this mission.

321
00:13:25,350 --> 00:13:27,570
We wanna make sure that we are good partners in this space,

322
00:13:27,570 --> 00:13:29,430
that we are not trying to own the work.

323
00:13:29,430 --> 00:13:30,540
Everything that we develop,

324
00:13:30,540 --> 00:13:32,850
everything that we put out is licensed

325
00:13:32,850 --> 00:13:34,740
through Creative Commons, everything is open source.

326
00:13:34,740 --> 00:13:37,290
We really want to be a good partner

327
00:13:37,290 --> 00:13:38,790
and a catalyst for this change

328
00:13:38,790 --> 00:13:42,510
and human-centered change for that matter.

329
00:13:42,510 --> 00:13:46,230
And so we establish these partnerships

330
00:13:46,230 --> 00:13:47,097
in order to make sure that students

331
00:13:47,097 --> 00:13:49,020
are on a path to AI readiness,

332
00:13:49,020 --> 00:13:52,710
and that necessitates systemic readiness,

333
00:13:52,710 --> 00:13:55,170
that necessitates educator readiness itself.

334
00:13:55,170 --> 00:13:58,587
And it cannot just be something that's a tool

335
00:13:58,587 --> 00:14:00,690
and a technology tool that you implement right away

336
00:14:00,690 --> 00:14:03,090
and you say, the AI box is checked off.

337
00:14:03,090 --> 00:14:04,470
We're gonna make sure that we're actually addressing

338
00:14:04,470 --> 00:14:07,170
systemic change that needs to be addressed.

339
00:14:07,170 --> 00:14:08,700
So thank you and we'll get into discussion

340
00:14:08,700 --> 00:14:10,389
a little bit later.

341
00:14:10,389 --> 00:14:13,389
(audience clapping)

342
00:14:14,970 --> 00:14:17,253
- Next is Summer from Skillfully.

343
00:14:21,030 --> 00:14:23,430
- Hello, everyone and good afternoon.

344
00:14:23,430 --> 00:14:24,375
As Alicia mentioned,

345
00:14:24,375 --> 00:14:25,208
my name is Summer

346
00:14:25,208 --> 00:14:27,870
and I'm the Head of Partnerships at Skillfully

347
00:14:27,870 --> 00:14:29,580
and I'm really excited to share

348
00:14:29,580 --> 00:14:33,450
some of the exciting work

349
00:14:33,450 --> 00:14:35,580
that we're doing with a number of colleges

350
00:14:35,580 --> 00:14:38,080
within the California Community College ecosystem.

351
00:14:38,970 --> 00:14:41,740
Really the north star of Skillfully

352
00:14:43,620 --> 00:14:47,340
is to enhance students' career opportunities

353
00:14:47,340 --> 00:14:50,970
through experiential learning and work integrated learning.

354
00:14:50,970 --> 00:14:53,220
And I'll tell you a little bit about how we do this

355
00:14:53,220 --> 00:14:54,920
and I'll try to keep this concise.

356
00:14:55,860 --> 00:14:58,110
So we're an AI platform

357
00:14:58,110 --> 00:15:01,860
that is basically enabling a digital twin

358
00:15:01,860 --> 00:15:04,170
of the skills that you see reflected

359
00:15:04,170 --> 00:15:07,350
in the ever evolving skills landscape

360
00:15:07,350 --> 00:15:09,300
within the industry today.

361
00:15:09,300 --> 00:15:11,220
What we're able to do and offer students

362
00:15:11,220 --> 00:15:14,190
is the ability to use our AI tooling

363
00:15:14,190 --> 00:15:16,090
to build out simulations

364
00:15:17,770 --> 00:15:22,230
that are a direct representation of a particular job

365
00:15:22,230 --> 00:15:25,680
or a particular suite of industry skills

366
00:15:25,680 --> 00:15:28,690
basically allowing that experiential learning

367
00:15:30,687 --> 00:15:32,220
for those particular skills

368
00:15:32,220 --> 00:15:35,430
to enable career readiness for students.

369
00:15:35,430 --> 00:15:37,170
Students are, through our platform,

370
00:15:37,170 --> 00:15:41,490
able to not just showcase their skills through a resume,

371
00:15:41,490 --> 00:15:44,220
but also often or more often,

372
00:15:44,220 --> 00:15:46,800
so through the actual skills demonstrations

373
00:15:46,800 --> 00:15:48,600
through our simulations, which are scored

374
00:15:48,600 --> 00:15:51,393
and they're provided feedback and evaluation on.

375
00:15:52,230 --> 00:15:56,010
Our simulations also enable deeper career exploration.

376
00:15:56,010 --> 00:16:00,000
So when a student is deciding, hey is, you know,

377
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:02,640
am I interested in a career in sales or healthcare,

378
00:16:02,640 --> 00:16:05,250
it's hard to be able to know whether that's true

379
00:16:05,250 --> 00:16:07,680
unless they have actually had their hand in trying it.

380
00:16:07,680 --> 00:16:10,980
So what our simulations enable is a lever

381
00:16:10,980 --> 00:16:12,720
by which students can get exposure

382
00:16:12,720 --> 00:16:15,630
to these skills throughout their educational journey

383
00:16:15,630 --> 00:16:18,330
to see if a particular career trajectory

384
00:16:18,330 --> 00:16:20,103
is of actual interest to them.

385
00:16:21,030 --> 00:16:24,420
Our platform also delivers broader,

386
00:16:24,420 --> 00:16:26,700
overarching career readiness support

387
00:16:26,700 --> 00:16:30,480
in the form of wraparound interviewing,

388
00:16:30,480 --> 00:16:33,330
mentorship and networking support as well.

389
00:16:33,330 --> 00:16:34,470
And as I mentioned,

390
00:16:34,470 --> 00:16:37,080
all of these simulations are scored

391
00:16:37,080 --> 00:16:39,630
and we're giving narrative feedback to students

392
00:16:39,630 --> 00:16:43,050
so they could actually uplevel their skill proficiencies

393
00:16:43,050 --> 00:16:47,040
within both of these industry-specific skills domains

394
00:16:47,040 --> 00:16:49,590
in addition to the broader career skills

395
00:16:49,590 --> 00:16:51,040
that they'll need to succeed.

396
00:16:53,490 --> 00:16:56,040
I wanna talk a little bit about an interesting pilot

397
00:16:56,040 --> 00:16:56,873
that we're doing

398
00:16:56,873 --> 00:16:59,760
with the Bay Area Community College Consortium.

399
00:16:59,760 --> 00:17:02,280
We're really excited about this work

400
00:17:02,280 --> 00:17:07,280
and are excited to expand this work in the coming semesters.

401
00:17:07,320 --> 00:17:11,340
Currently we are working with some local

402
00:17:11,340 --> 00:17:16,020
big tech players, namely companies like Salesforce,

403
00:17:16,020 --> 00:17:18,690
to be able to take their job descriptions

404
00:17:18,690 --> 00:17:21,120
for their entry-level roles.

405
00:17:21,120 --> 00:17:22,710
We're doing a specific emphasis

406
00:17:22,710 --> 00:17:24,810
on tech sales roles right now

407
00:17:24,810 --> 00:17:27,060
and we are building out digital twins

408
00:17:27,060 --> 00:17:28,710
of those job descriptions

409
00:17:28,710 --> 00:17:31,680
to put those in front of students for two purposes.

410
00:17:31,680 --> 00:17:35,070
One, skill development and skill exposure.

411
00:17:35,070 --> 00:17:37,290
And also for us to be able to validate

412
00:17:37,290 --> 00:17:40,050
and evaluate those students

413
00:17:40,050 --> 00:17:42,240
for opportunities for actual apprenticeship

414
00:17:42,240 --> 00:17:45,603
or full-time opportunities that those employers might have.

415
00:17:47,730 --> 00:17:48,900
As I mentioned,

416
00:17:48,900 --> 00:17:51,960
we want to make sure that we're democratizing access

417
00:17:51,960 --> 00:17:54,480
to not just the students who might be applying

418
00:17:54,480 --> 00:17:55,980
within that particular program

419
00:17:55,980 --> 00:17:58,710
or within that particular hiring cycle.

420
00:17:58,710 --> 00:18:01,140
So we make these available to a number

421
00:18:01,140 --> 00:18:04,680
of the different colleges across the BACCC ecosystem

422
00:18:04,680 --> 00:18:08,280
so that students can practice these skill simulations

423
00:18:08,280 --> 00:18:09,990
and get that feedback that they need

424
00:18:09,990 --> 00:18:12,660
to be able to further develop these skills

425
00:18:12,660 --> 00:18:15,690
for future career opportunities.

426
00:18:15,690 --> 00:18:18,930
Some future directions that we're exploring for this work.

427
00:18:18,930 --> 00:18:23,930
We'd like to expand outside of just this tech sales hub

428
00:18:24,060 --> 00:18:26,910
to other industries like healthcare.

429
00:18:26,910 --> 00:18:31,200
Also looking at computer science careers as well.

430
00:18:31,200 --> 00:18:34,620
And also, as my friends on stage today,

431
00:18:34,620 --> 00:18:38,130
looking at enabling AI literacy and fluency

432
00:18:38,130 --> 00:18:40,863
for students across the California College ecosystem.

433
00:18:42,090 --> 00:18:43,980
I think my five minutes is up.

434
00:18:43,980 --> 00:18:45,000
Thank you for your patience there

435
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:47,640
and if you're interested in working with us,

436
00:18:47,640 --> 00:18:50,940
I put my email address up on the screen here.

437
00:18:50,940 --> 00:18:53,910
Also, I'll be hanging out a little bit

438
00:18:53,910 --> 00:18:55,020
after this presentation,

439
00:18:55,020 --> 00:18:56,820
so feel free to come and talk to me.

440
00:18:58,410 --> 00:18:59,925
All right, thank you so much.

441
00:18:59,925 --> 00:19:02,925
(audience clapping)

442
00:19:05,340 --> 00:19:06,690
- Thank you, everybody.

443
00:19:06,690 --> 00:19:09,360
We're gonna go ahead and ask the panelists questions

444
00:19:09,360 --> 00:19:12,427
starting off with Tara from Ourmedia.

445
00:19:12,427 --> 00:19:16,770
"So your work at Ourmedia centers on partnering with Gen Z

446
00:19:16,770 --> 00:19:19,860
to identify AI literacy and career readiness needs

447
00:19:19,860 --> 00:19:22,950
and use cases in order to inform faculty

448
00:19:22,950 --> 00:19:25,800
and administrative responses to AI's impact

449
00:19:25,800 --> 00:19:28,830
on learning and career environment.

450
00:19:28,830 --> 00:19:31,410
How should students' attitudes inform

451
00:19:31,410 --> 00:19:33,960
the development of responses to AI

452
00:19:33,960 --> 00:19:36,447
in the learning and career?"

453
00:19:38,610 --> 00:19:39,443
- Thank you.

454
00:19:40,800 --> 00:19:41,633
Well, I mean,

455
00:19:41,633 --> 00:19:43,800
I'll start by sharing my opinion

456
00:19:43,800 --> 00:19:45,630
that student voices should be involved

457
00:19:45,630 --> 00:19:48,840
at every level of learning design, but...

458
00:19:48,840 --> 00:19:51,624
Oh, thank you. I'm like, you can clap for that. (laughs)

459
00:19:51,624 --> 00:19:52,920
(audience laughing)

460
00:19:52,920 --> 00:19:56,590
But I think that AI specifically introduces

461
00:19:57,810 --> 00:19:59,070
really chaos, honestly,

462
00:19:59,070 --> 00:20:02,100
introduces chaos to the traditional learning model

463
00:20:02,100 --> 00:20:05,070
in ways that really, really kind of increase

464
00:20:05,070 --> 00:20:08,610
the need to right now be digging in

465
00:20:08,610 --> 00:20:10,920
with the students directly.

466
00:20:10,920 --> 00:20:13,710
One statistic I saw recently that just kind of blew me away,

467
00:20:13,710 --> 00:20:16,410
I think it was from, I wanna say the World Economic Forum,

468
00:20:16,410 --> 00:20:20,190
was that 49% of Gen Zs who had been surveyed

469
00:20:20,190 --> 00:20:22,470
who'd recently attained a degree

470
00:20:22,470 --> 00:20:25,590
reported feeling that what they were seeing around them

471
00:20:25,590 --> 00:20:28,173
with AI meant that their degree had less value.

472
00:20:29,070 --> 00:20:31,710
So I think one really fundamental question

473
00:20:31,710 --> 00:20:34,620
that's being asked right now in just across

474
00:20:34,620 --> 00:20:37,530
a lot of higher ed spaces is what are the skills

475
00:20:37,530 --> 00:20:39,210
that young people need to be equipped with

476
00:20:39,210 --> 00:20:41,850
to succeed in this AI-driven future of work?

477
00:20:41,850 --> 00:20:43,710
And then how are we as institutions equipping

478
00:20:43,710 --> 00:20:45,180
them with those skills?

479
00:20:45,180 --> 00:20:47,370
I think that starting with student attitudes

480
00:20:47,370 --> 00:20:50,340
to then find practical solutions

481
00:20:50,340 --> 00:20:52,290
that can support them is the way to go

482
00:20:52,290 --> 00:20:55,980
because when students are expressing fear or anxiety

483
00:20:55,980 --> 00:20:58,800
or on the flip side curiosity or excitement,

484
00:20:58,800 --> 00:21:01,020
those are really important cues that tell us, okay,

485
00:21:01,020 --> 00:21:03,990
here are potentially really important needs that they have

486
00:21:03,990 --> 00:21:06,180
or here area areas that they just don't have

487
00:21:06,180 --> 00:21:08,130
any comfort or familiarity in.

488
00:21:08,130 --> 00:21:09,330
So maybe this is where we need

489
00:21:09,330 --> 00:21:11,280
to beef up the support that we're giving.

490
00:21:11,280 --> 00:21:13,770
So yeah, all of that to say,

491
00:21:13,770 --> 00:21:14,910
I think it's always important

492
00:21:14,910 --> 00:21:17,760
to look at student attitudes to get cues when we're dealing

493
00:21:17,760 --> 00:21:20,580
with something that's as transformative as AI,

494
00:21:20,580 --> 00:21:23,040
but especially right now as we're seeing

495
00:21:23,040 --> 00:21:25,260
that like the career ladder

496
00:21:25,260 --> 00:21:26,880
is gonna be chopped off at the bottom,

497
00:21:26,880 --> 00:21:28,230
entry level roles are gonna be

498
00:21:28,230 --> 00:21:30,390
potentially going away in a lot of spaces.

499
00:21:30,390 --> 00:21:32,010
And so we need to sit down

500
00:21:32,010 --> 00:21:34,260
and all of these questions that were

501
00:21:34,260 --> 00:21:35,430
on this side of education

502
00:21:35,430 --> 00:21:37,890
asking the students are asking them too.

503
00:21:37,890 --> 00:21:39,420
And I think starting that dialogue

504
00:21:39,420 --> 00:21:41,020
is the best way to move forward.

505
00:21:43,860 --> 00:21:47,520
- Christian, "Your work at the AI Education Project

506
00:21:47,520 --> 00:21:49,320
centers on providing educators

507
00:21:49,320 --> 00:21:51,510
with the tools they need to help students thrive

508
00:21:51,510 --> 00:21:54,210
in a world where AI is everywhere.

509
00:21:54,210 --> 00:21:57,330
What are the biggest challenges that educators are facing

510
00:21:57,330 --> 00:22:00,087
in ensuring their students are AI proficient?"

511
00:22:01,650 --> 00:22:03,780
- I mean, as I mentioned,

512
00:22:03,780 --> 00:22:06,657
the majority of my work is with K-12 systems,

513
00:22:06,657 --> 00:22:08,707
and I can tell you right off the bat that

514
00:22:10,830 --> 00:22:11,663
teachers in K-12 systems don't see a lot of support

515
00:22:11,663 --> 00:22:15,090
coming from the system itself or them,

516
00:22:15,090 --> 00:22:17,160
and AI looks very different depending

517
00:22:17,160 --> 00:22:18,690
on the students that they're serving,

518
00:22:18,690 --> 00:22:22,140
meaning what a third grade teacher is experiencing

519
00:22:22,140 --> 00:22:23,310
is gonna be extremely different

520
00:22:23,310 --> 00:22:25,560
from what a 10th grade teacher is gonna be experiencing

521
00:22:25,560 --> 00:22:28,020
and there is no solution that will work

522
00:22:28,020 --> 00:22:29,490
for both of those teachers.

523
00:22:29,490 --> 00:22:30,990
And school systems are right now

524
00:22:30,990 --> 00:22:33,070
are having a hard time understanding

525
00:22:33,930 --> 00:22:36,180
what those support systems need to look like,

526
00:22:37,020 --> 00:22:39,240
what teachers actually are needing.

527
00:22:39,240 --> 00:22:41,280
Concurrently, and this is something I'm sure

528
00:22:41,280 --> 00:22:44,700
all of you can agree with,

529
00:22:44,700 --> 00:22:48,660
is we see a lot of a push from EdTech.

530
00:22:48,660 --> 00:22:51,000
Like, I am making assumptions here,

531
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:52,410
but I'm assuming the marketing budgets

532
00:22:52,410 --> 00:22:54,720
for a lot of EdTech companies has gone up substantially

533
00:22:54,720 --> 00:22:56,910
because at the same time we see teachers being marketed

534
00:22:56,910 --> 00:22:59,730
to by a lot of EdTech tools, platforms,

535
00:22:59,730 --> 00:23:02,070
websites that are AI powered

536
00:23:02,070 --> 00:23:05,250
and there's a lot of noise out there

537
00:23:05,250 --> 00:23:07,020
in terms of what a solution can look like

538
00:23:07,020 --> 00:23:08,580
or what a solution should be.

539
00:23:08,580 --> 00:23:09,990
And so with all of that noise

540
00:23:09,990 --> 00:23:13,200
and with all of the disruption that's happening

541
00:23:13,200 --> 00:23:16,320
both to students and teachers in their personal lives

542
00:23:16,320 --> 00:23:18,510
and in their professional lives,

543
00:23:18,510 --> 00:23:20,490
I think teachers are kind of like,

544
00:23:20,490 --> 00:23:22,080
either putting their head in the sand

545
00:23:22,080 --> 00:23:24,540
because navigating this world is a little bit too much

546
00:23:24,540 --> 00:23:26,730
and they're already a little bit overworked

547
00:23:26,730 --> 00:23:29,880
and/or they're looking for the one thing

548
00:23:29,880 --> 00:23:31,530
that will save them time or the one thing

549
00:23:31,530 --> 00:23:33,780
that they think is gonna be the silver bullet.

550
00:23:33,780 --> 00:23:34,613
And that's where a lot

551
00:23:34,613 --> 00:23:36,660
of these marketing campaigns are working.

552
00:23:36,660 --> 00:23:39,090
And that's what you see adoption for AI tools

553
00:23:39,090 --> 00:23:42,300
so broad is because there are real needs out there

554
00:23:42,300 --> 00:23:45,180
that teachers are experiencing.

555
00:23:45,180 --> 00:23:47,830
And a lot of times AI tools can

556
00:23:48,870 --> 00:23:51,570
promise solutions to those needs.

557
00:23:51,570 --> 00:23:54,960
I think this idea of offloading

558
00:23:54,960 --> 00:23:56,730
a solutions to those needs from the system

559
00:23:56,730 --> 00:23:59,730
to individual tools is something I don't want to see.

560
00:23:59,730 --> 00:24:01,980
Something I would love to see more of is like,

561
00:24:01,980 --> 00:24:05,490
how can systems support teachers a little bit more directly?

562
00:24:05,490 --> 00:24:08,370
How can systems offer those resources,

563
00:24:08,370 --> 00:24:13,050
those, I guess, like guidance to help cut through noise?

564
00:24:13,050 --> 00:24:14,940
I don't need another 50-page report.

565
00:24:14,940 --> 00:24:16,020
I need like a two-page,

566
00:24:16,020 --> 00:24:18,200
like really quick thing about what I need to do right now

567
00:24:18,200 --> 00:24:20,070
in the next semester of my work

568
00:24:20,070 --> 00:24:24,000
in order to actually address student readiness.

569
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:24,990
And the next semester,

570
00:24:24,990 --> 00:24:26,940
what are the next few things that our system

571
00:24:26,940 --> 00:24:28,620
is working on that I need to implement now?

572
00:24:28,620 --> 00:24:30,060
I think making it bite-sized,

573
00:24:30,060 --> 00:24:31,980
making it immediately actionable

574
00:24:31,980 --> 00:24:35,130
and making support actually responsive to teacher needs

575
00:24:35,130 --> 00:24:37,553
is something that's really, really necessary right now.

576
00:24:41,490 --> 00:24:45,030
- Question is for Summer. Let's see.

577
00:24:45,030 --> 00:24:46,657
I'm sorry, lemme get...

578
00:24:46,657 --> 00:24:49,620
"While Ourmedia and the AI Education Project

579
00:24:49,620 --> 00:24:52,320
are providing tools for AI literacy to students,

580
00:24:52,320 --> 00:24:54,630
Skillfully provides AI-driven technology

581
00:24:54,630 --> 00:24:56,970
to ensure that students and job seekers

582
00:24:56,970 --> 00:24:58,800
are ready for the workforce.

583
00:24:58,800 --> 00:25:02,520
At a time when the skills employers demands

584
00:25:02,520 --> 00:25:03,810
are changing rapidly,

585
00:25:03,810 --> 00:25:06,540
how can we make sure higher ed is staying

586
00:25:06,540 --> 00:25:09,060
in step with changing industry skills?

587
00:25:09,060 --> 00:25:12,207
What are employers looking for as skills change?"

588
00:25:13,770 --> 00:25:15,060
- Thank you for that question, Alicia,

589
00:25:15,060 --> 00:25:18,210
and I'll tackle the first portion of that question

590
00:25:18,210 --> 00:25:22,830
just around the rapidly changing skills landscape

591
00:25:22,830 --> 00:25:25,203
that's happening out in the job market today.

592
00:25:26,340 --> 00:25:27,960
My colleagues have talked a lot

593
00:25:27,960 --> 00:25:32,520
about how AI is disrupting the skills landscape

594
00:25:32,520 --> 00:25:35,340
and how things are rapidly changing,

595
00:25:35,340 --> 00:25:38,550
wiping out entry level roles.

596
00:25:38,550 --> 00:25:39,870
And in addition,

597
00:25:39,870 --> 00:25:41,970
we also have employers who are turning

598
00:25:41,970 --> 00:25:44,370
to more skills-based hiring approaches

599
00:25:44,370 --> 00:25:46,800
where they're really trying to make sure

600
00:25:46,800 --> 00:25:49,770
there's a precise match between a student

601
00:25:49,770 --> 00:25:53,520
or a job seeker skills and what they're hiring for.

602
00:25:53,520 --> 00:25:55,920
So the question of how do we make sure

603
00:25:55,920 --> 00:25:59,130
that higher ed is able to stay in parallel

604
00:25:59,130 --> 00:26:01,800
or in step with those employer demands,

605
00:26:01,800 --> 00:26:04,500
which ultimately, whether we like it or not,

606
00:26:04,500 --> 00:26:05,460
those are the arbiters

607
00:26:05,460 --> 00:26:09,420
of who ultimately does enter the workforce.

608
00:26:09,420 --> 00:26:12,130
We just need to have more collaboration

609
00:26:13,650 --> 00:26:16,560
between employers and higher ed.

610
00:26:16,560 --> 00:26:18,390
And I know that I'm preaching to the choir, (laughs)

611
00:26:18,390 --> 00:26:21,330
so I know that this is not like a profound statement.

612
00:26:21,330 --> 00:26:23,280
I can share a little bit about some of the work

613
00:26:23,280 --> 00:26:25,410
that we're doing with other partners

614
00:26:25,410 --> 00:26:27,690
where we're bringing employers to the table

615
00:26:27,690 --> 00:26:30,960
to be active participants and helping to relay

616
00:26:30,960 --> 00:26:34,230
and translate how skills are changing in the workforce

617
00:26:34,230 --> 00:26:36,690
so that we can then translate that in the form

618
00:26:36,690 --> 00:26:41,430
of our technology to the students, job seekers,

619
00:26:41,430 --> 00:26:44,040
and learners that we're working in our partnerships

620
00:26:44,040 --> 00:26:47,400
with workforce development organizations, higher ed,

621
00:26:47,400 --> 00:26:51,063
and in some cases K-12 CTE programming.

622
00:26:53,340 --> 00:26:56,100
I wish I had like a silver bullet to say it's like easy,

623
00:26:56,100 --> 00:26:59,370
but a lot of this is like starting from the ground up,

624
00:26:59,370 --> 00:27:01,080
re-imagining what's possible,

625
00:27:01,080 --> 00:27:02,880
having multiple conversations

626
00:27:02,880 --> 00:27:04,980
with employer and industry partners

627
00:27:04,980 --> 00:27:06,840
to help translate that skills data.

628
00:27:06,840 --> 00:27:09,960
And then making sure as ed tech providers

629
00:27:09,960 --> 00:27:13,110
we're actually being responsive to that.

630
00:27:13,110 --> 00:27:14,730
Not just like putting something shiny

631
00:27:14,730 --> 00:27:17,040
and flashy out there for the sake of marketing, but like,

632
00:27:17,040 --> 00:27:18,960
we are solving a real problem

633
00:27:18,960 --> 00:27:21,390
and helping us to bridge this skills gap

634
00:27:21,390 --> 00:27:23,760
and this chasm that's widening

635
00:27:23,760 --> 00:27:26,190
and even more rapidly than it was say,

636
00:27:26,190 --> 00:27:28,110
you know, five years ago.

637
00:27:28,110 --> 00:27:28,943
And, sorry,

638
00:27:28,943 --> 00:27:30,120
I think there was one more piece of that question

639
00:27:30,120 --> 00:27:31,170
around what skills

640
00:27:31,170 --> 00:27:32,870
are employers looking for? - Yes.

641
00:27:33,810 --> 00:27:37,770
- While we have done a lot of work on making sure

642
00:27:37,770 --> 00:27:39,960
that we are bridging those skills for the, you know,

643
00:27:39,960 --> 00:27:43,140
industry-relevant or role-specific skills

644
00:27:43,140 --> 00:27:45,210
that employers are looking for,

645
00:27:45,210 --> 00:27:48,060
a huge thing that we hear from both the, you know,

646
00:27:48,060 --> 00:27:49,500
our career service partners

647
00:27:49,500 --> 00:27:51,780
and from the employers that we work with is

648
00:27:51,780 --> 00:27:54,330
we can't leave the durable skills behind, right?

649
00:27:54,330 --> 00:27:57,300
So we have to make sure that when students are showing up

650
00:27:57,300 --> 00:28:00,150
for interviews, they're able to hold eye contact,

651
00:28:00,150 --> 00:28:02,820
they're able to articulate their transferable skills

652
00:28:02,820 --> 00:28:05,430
that they've gathered throughout their career

653
00:28:05,430 --> 00:28:08,910
or through their, you know, past lived experiences.

654
00:28:08,910 --> 00:28:10,350
And so I just wanna make sure

655
00:28:10,350 --> 00:28:11,790
that we're not just focusing on, okay,

656
00:28:11,790 --> 00:28:13,170
these industry skills are changing

657
00:28:13,170 --> 00:28:15,180
or this is what you now need to be successful

658
00:28:15,180 --> 00:28:18,360
in this particular role in the sector.

659
00:28:18,360 --> 00:28:22,200
But also just making sure we're really helping to uplevel

660
00:28:22,200 --> 00:28:25,020
and maintain those critical durable skills as well

661
00:28:25,020 --> 00:28:26,583
for students and job seekers.

662
00:28:27,690 --> 00:28:30,637
- Thank you. Back to Tara from Ourmedia.

663
00:28:30,637 --> 00:28:32,910
"What insights have you gathered from students

664
00:28:32,910 --> 00:28:35,130
regarding Ourmedia's recent report

665
00:28:35,130 --> 00:28:38,040
piloting students-centered AI engagement

666
00:28:38,040 --> 00:28:40,347
across California Community Colleges?"

667
00:28:42,930 --> 00:28:44,973
- Yeah, so I mean, I think,

668
00:28:46,860 --> 00:28:49,050
I'll offer a statement and offer a few themes.

669
00:28:49,050 --> 00:28:50,070
The statement would be

670
00:28:50,070 --> 00:28:53,490
that there is no one consistent student perspective

671
00:28:53,490 --> 00:28:55,473
or relationship with AI.

672
00:28:56,670 --> 00:28:58,590
I've interacted over the past couple of years

673
00:28:58,590 --> 00:29:01,290
with students who say, "I will never ever touch AI

674
00:29:01,290 --> 00:29:03,300
no matter how you offer it to me."

675
00:29:03,300 --> 00:29:05,107
And I've talked to other students who are saying,

676
00:29:05,107 --> 00:29:07,860
"I don't know how I would've learned without AI."

677
00:29:07,860 --> 00:29:09,870
And there's a wide spectrum in between those.

678
00:29:09,870 --> 00:29:11,760
So that's kind of the overall thing.

679
00:29:11,760 --> 00:29:13,740
But then within that, there is some themes

680
00:29:13,740 --> 00:29:15,890
that I've definitely been noticing recently

681
00:29:17,010 --> 00:29:18,570
and a lot of them are reflected in the...

682
00:29:18,570 --> 00:29:20,610
So to drill in a little more

683
00:29:20,610 --> 00:29:22,410
into what the spring effort looked like,

684
00:29:22,410 --> 00:29:24,720
we hosted that workshop with students.

685
00:29:24,720 --> 00:29:27,570
We also hosted a panel of student leaders

686
00:29:27,570 --> 00:29:29,610
as well as doing some student interviews.

687
00:29:29,610 --> 00:29:31,590
So we got a really kind of holistic view

688
00:29:31,590 --> 00:29:33,150
of the student perspective.

689
00:29:33,150 --> 00:29:35,130
One really big theme that stood out to me

690
00:29:35,130 --> 00:29:36,997
is that a lot of students were saying,

691
00:29:36,997 --> 00:29:39,570
"I'm really concerned about AI's impacts

692
00:29:39,570 --> 00:29:41,610
on my critical thinking skills.

693
00:29:41,610 --> 00:29:43,920
I'm really concerned that I'm gonna develop a dependency

694
00:29:43,920 --> 00:29:48,690
on AI that makes it hard for me to be creative or efficient

695
00:29:48,690 --> 00:29:52,290
or collaborative on my own."

696
00:29:52,290 --> 00:29:54,630
Some students were saying, "I see this with my own self."

697
00:29:54,630 --> 00:29:57,510
One quote that came to me from a student,

698
00:29:57,510 --> 00:29:58,560
actually not in the spring,

699
00:29:58,560 --> 00:30:00,990
but even before that was, "When I use AI,

700
00:30:00,990 --> 00:30:03,960
sometimes it feels like I'm turning my brain off."

701
00:30:03,960 --> 00:30:05,220
That was definitely concerning.

702
00:30:05,220 --> 00:30:07,800
Some of them are saying, "When I look around at my peers,

703
00:30:07,800 --> 00:30:09,420
I'm worried that they're relying on tools

704
00:30:09,420 --> 00:30:10,590
that I'm not relying on,

705
00:30:10,590 --> 00:30:12,000
and they're not gonna be able to keep up

706
00:30:12,000 --> 00:30:13,290
with me when it really comes down

707
00:30:13,290 --> 00:30:14,700
to actually doing the work."

708
00:30:14,700 --> 00:30:17,790
So that's the first thing is really, really concerned about

709
00:30:17,790 --> 00:30:19,770
where is the boundary for how much

710
00:30:19,770 --> 00:30:22,590
and to what extent I should be engaging with AI.

711
00:30:22,590 --> 00:30:25,023
The second theme that stood out to me was,

712
00:30:27,180 --> 00:30:28,410
how is AI gonna change

713
00:30:28,410 --> 00:30:29,910
what it looks like for me to get a job?

714
00:30:29,910 --> 00:30:32,790
I think that's the biggest fear that students have

715
00:30:32,790 --> 00:30:34,230
'cause they're seeing the headlines.

716
00:30:34,230 --> 00:30:37,560
There are massive layoffs in the tech industry,

717
00:30:37,560 --> 00:30:40,170
but elsewhere, I think I saw something

718
00:30:40,170 --> 00:30:43,200
that said that two-thirds of hiring managers say

719
00:30:43,200 --> 00:30:45,990
that they wouldn't consider hiring someone

720
00:30:45,990 --> 00:30:47,340
who's not AI literate.

721
00:30:47,340 --> 00:30:50,190
So the students are hearing these headlines and the signals

722
00:30:50,190 --> 00:30:51,420
and they're concerned.

723
00:30:51,420 --> 00:30:52,830
They're like, "I know this will impact

724
00:30:52,830 --> 00:30:54,990
my ability to get a job."

725
00:30:54,990 --> 00:30:57,390
The third piece, after those first two,

726
00:30:57,390 --> 00:31:01,740
is that students don't seem to have a consistent narrative

727
00:31:01,740 --> 00:31:03,480
coming to them from their educators

728
00:31:03,480 --> 00:31:05,850
about how to deal with those first two pieces.

729
00:31:05,850 --> 00:31:07,080
So when it comes to making sure

730
00:31:07,080 --> 00:31:09,660
that they're not developing over reliance on AI

731
00:31:09,660 --> 00:31:11,670
or on the flip side, if they're trying to prepare

732
00:31:11,670 --> 00:31:13,710
to succeed in the future of work,

733
00:31:13,710 --> 00:31:16,567
a really, really common concern that I heard was,

734
00:31:16,567 --> 00:31:18,810
"Some of my professors are saying to use it,

735
00:31:18,810 --> 00:31:19,860
some of them aren't.

736
00:31:19,860 --> 00:31:23,100
And I don't know how to make sense of all of this."

737
00:31:23,100 --> 00:31:26,100
So I think that's at the heart of what we're trying

738
00:31:26,100 --> 00:31:27,150
to do with our approach

739
00:31:27,150 --> 00:31:30,960
of bringing the student voices into dialogue with faculty

740
00:31:30,960 --> 00:31:33,070
and administrators is to make sure that

741
00:31:34,770 --> 00:31:37,140
from the educator perspective, we're well equipped

742
00:31:37,140 --> 00:31:39,690
with hear how my students are engaging with AI,

743
00:31:39,690 --> 00:31:42,510
hear what their needs ares, hear what their fears are,

744
00:31:42,510 --> 00:31:44,730
and here's how I deliver a consistent message

745
00:31:44,730 --> 00:31:46,560
that helps them deal with that.

746
00:31:46,560 --> 00:31:49,920
So yeah, I feel like I talked for a while, but.

747
00:31:49,920 --> 00:31:50,753
- Thank you.

748
00:31:52,093 --> 00:31:54,360
Next question here for Christian,

749
00:31:54,360 --> 00:31:56,433
from the AI Education Project,

750
00:31:58,100 --> 00:32:00,990
"Where are you seeing the most success in working

751
00:32:00,990 --> 00:32:04,707
with educators to embed AI literacy into their curriculum?"

752
00:32:07,710 --> 00:32:09,720
- This one working? - Yes.

753
00:32:09,720 --> 00:32:12,420
- Cool, this mic is really cute, so I'll use this one.

754
00:32:14,670 --> 00:32:18,420
So the thing about AI literacy work

755
00:32:18,420 --> 00:32:21,690
is that you don't want it to feel like an extra thing

756
00:32:21,690 --> 00:32:23,310
that educators have to do

757
00:32:23,310 --> 00:32:25,080
'cause I think educator's workloads

758
00:32:25,080 --> 00:32:27,960
and the objectives that they have in their courses

759
00:32:27,960 --> 00:32:31,770
and their curricula is already quite overloaded.

760
00:32:31,770 --> 00:32:33,900
And so the most success

761
00:32:33,900 --> 00:32:35,790
that you have in helping educators actually build

762
00:32:35,790 --> 00:32:38,610
student AI literacy is in the opportunities you have

763
00:32:38,610 --> 00:32:40,620
to embed those learning objectives

764
00:32:40,620 --> 00:32:42,903
in already existing learning objectives.

765
00:32:44,100 --> 00:32:46,530
And I like to reference this a lot,

766
00:32:46,530 --> 00:32:51,090
it's quite akin to the internet, where the internet

767
00:32:51,090 --> 00:32:52,410
is something that's quite ubiquitous,

768
00:32:52,410 --> 00:32:53,550
it's changed to work,

769
00:32:53,550 --> 00:32:56,550
it's really disrupted a lot of industries.

770
00:32:56,550 --> 00:32:58,980
In your courses, in your classes,

771
00:32:58,980 --> 00:33:00,990
we don't have internet learning objectives

772
00:33:00,990 --> 00:33:04,920
that are siloed into like an internet category.

773
00:33:04,920 --> 00:33:07,590
Similarly, I don't think we need AI learning objectives

774
00:33:07,590 --> 00:33:10,440
that are siloed into an AI category.

775
00:33:10,440 --> 00:33:13,470
Like we embed internet literacy work naturally

776
00:33:13,470 --> 00:33:15,000
because it's a part of the way that we work,

777
00:33:15,000 --> 00:33:16,680
it's a part of the way that we share information,

778
00:33:16,680 --> 00:33:19,809
it's a part of the way that we even learn today

779
00:33:19,809 --> 00:33:20,970
in the real world.

780
00:33:20,970 --> 00:33:22,770
I think the same thing's gonna happen with AI.

781
00:33:22,770 --> 00:33:26,460
We need to embed AI literacy into the existing work streams

782
00:33:26,460 --> 00:33:27,600
that are happening.

783
00:33:27,600 --> 00:33:30,240
If in classes teachers are pursuing

784
00:33:30,240 --> 00:33:31,710
project-based learning opportunities,

785
00:33:31,710 --> 00:33:33,870
how can you embed the use of AI tools

786
00:33:33,870 --> 00:33:37,380
in those learning opportunities to embed AI literacy

787
00:33:37,380 --> 00:33:39,030
as part of one of those learning objectives?

788
00:33:39,030 --> 00:33:41,970
Even though it might be a project on world languages,

789
00:33:41,970 --> 00:33:44,040
you could still think about how students can use

790
00:33:44,040 --> 00:33:48,270
or think about AI as it pertains to that project itself.

791
00:33:48,270 --> 00:33:49,980
And so I would say the most success is when teachers

792
00:33:49,980 --> 00:33:52,890
don't feel like AI is something that's like put onto them

793
00:33:52,890 --> 00:33:56,850
as an additional layer that's on a stack of objectives

794
00:33:56,850 --> 00:33:58,530
that they already have to teach against.

795
00:33:58,530 --> 00:34:00,390
But when it's a part or embedded

796
00:34:00,390 --> 00:34:02,590
within already existing learning objectives,

797
00:34:02,590 --> 00:34:05,370
it is not gonna take extra time out of their classroom

798
00:34:05,370 --> 00:34:06,990
or out of their syllabus,

799
00:34:06,990 --> 00:34:08,940
it's not gonna take extra units

800
00:34:08,940 --> 00:34:11,490
of lesson planning and delivery.

801
00:34:11,490 --> 00:34:12,570
I think that's where you can do it.

